<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Diego de Landa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1524">1524</a>–<a href="/years/1579">1579</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Diego de Landa was a Spanish Franciscan friar and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Yucatán. In 1562 he led an inquisition against Maya converts suspected of idolatry and burnt Maya codices and images at Maní. His account of Yucatán, the Relación de las cosas de Yucatán, nevertheless became a principal source for Maya history, customs and writing, including the provinces formed after the fall of Mayapan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diego-de-landa.jpg" alt="Diego de Landa" width="320" height="317" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diego_de_Landa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Diego de Landa Calderón was born on 12 November 1524 in Cifuentes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalajara-mexico">Guadalajara</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and became a Franciscan friar in 1541. He was sent to Yucatán as one of the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a>, arriving in 1549, to bring the Roman Catholic faith to the Maya peoples after the Spanish conquest. The Franciscan order held a spiritual monopoly granted by the Spanish crown, and Landa worked to strengthen its power and convert the Maya.</p><p>His first post was the mission of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-antonio">San Antonio</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/izamal">Izamal</a>, which remained his main residence. According to Cogolludo, his chief Franciscan biographer, he walked the length of the peninsula preaching in remote villages, and at Cupules he came upon a group of 300 about to sacrifice a boy, whom he released.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After hearing that Catholic Maya continued to worship idols, Landa ordered an Inquisition at Maní, which ended on 12 July 1562 with an auto de fé in which a disputed number of Maya codices, 27 books according to Landa, and about 5,000 alleged cult images were burned. The Inquisition used physical abuse: Maya nobles were jailed pending interrogation, and many nobles and commoners were hoisted by their bound hands, often with stone weights on the ankles or lashes to the back.</p><p>At his later trial Landa denied that any deaths or injuries directly resulted. He believed in a large underground network of apostasy led by displaced native priests and held that a papal bull, Exponi nobis, justified his actions. Bishop Toral sent him back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> to stand trial, and he was strongly condemned before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-the-indies">Council of the Indies</a>. In 1569 a committee of doctors absolved him, and after Toral died in 1571 Philip II appointed Landa the second bishop of Yucatán.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Landa wrote the Relación de las cosas de Yucatán, a catalogue of Maya religion, language, culture and writing, around 1566 on his return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. The original is lost; the surviving text is an abridgement made around 1660, rediscovered in the 19th century and published in a French-Spanish edition by Charles Etienne Brasseur de Bourbourg in 1862. It is the main contemporary source for Maya history.</p><p>For the writing system Landa asked two literate informants of a ruling dynasty to write the sign for each letter of the Spanish alphabet. The result was later judged inaccurate, until in the 1950s the Soviet linguist Yuri Knorozov showed that it was not an alphabet but a syllabary, which proved instrumental in the decipherment.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Diego de Landa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>According to <b>Diego de Landa</b>, one son of the Cocom ruler survived the massacre of his family at Mayapan only because he was away trading in Honduras.</blockquote><a href="/empire/league-of-mayapan">League of Mayapan · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1562 the Franciscan friar <b>Diego de Landa</b> burned the Maya books he found at Maní in Yucatán as works of the devil; only four Maya books survive today, in Dresden, Madrid, Paris and Mexico City.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mesoamerican-religion">Mesoamerican religion · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Spanish friars such as <b>Diego de Landa</b> described the ceremonies of Yucatán even as they suppressed them.</blockquote><a href="/culture/maya-civilisation">Maya civilisation · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>The fall of Tenochtitlan in 1521 ended the Aztec state religion; friars destroyed temples, images and books, and in 1562 <b>Diego de Landa</b> burned the books and images of the Maya at Maní.</blockquote><a href="/essays/faiths-on-the-move">Faiths on the Move</a></li><li><blockquote>According to <b>Bishop Diego de Landa</b>, a priest of the Chel lineage at Mayapan foresaw its fall and, after its destruction around 1441, founded a new realm.</blockquote><a href="/state/ah-kin-chel">Ah Kin Chel</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1562 <b>Diego de Landa</b> held his notorious auto-da-fé at Maní, burning Maya books.</blockquote><a href="/state/mani">Maní</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cifuentes, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merida-yucatan">Mérida</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.79,-2.62&amp;year=1559" aria-label="Cifuentes on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4486 707 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Diego de Landa"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4936 977h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4936 977h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.79,-2.62&amp;year=1559">What was here in 1559? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
